@page "/docs/troubleshooting/redirect-after-post"
@attribute [FollowsModeSwitch]

@* NavigateTo during a static post: with BlazorDisableThrowNavigationException (set by the .NET 10
   Blazor Web App template) it records a redirect and code after it runs; without it, it throws a
   NavigationException that a catch-all swallows. On an interactive page it never throws and code
   after it runs. All checked in scratch pages; the fix in form-redirect-after-save in all three
   modes. *@

<ArticlePage Section="troubleshooting" Slug="redirect-after-post">
    <Lead>
        <Code>NavigateTo</Code> doesn't always stop your code. Make it the last thing the handler does, or
        <Code>return</Code> right after it, and keep it out of <Code>try</Code>/<Code>catch</Code>.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">Problem</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            After saving a form, the handler calls <Code>NavigateTo</Code> so that reloading the page doesn't post the form
            again. One of two things goes wrong:
        </TextBlock>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>The browser is redirected, but code after <Code>NavigateTo</Code> still runs, such as a save that should have been skipped.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>On a static page, it doesn't redirect, and shows your error message although the save worked.</ListItem>
        </List>
        <Code Block Language="csharp" Title="Rename.razor" HighlightLines="6,13,15">@Broken</Code>

        <Heading Level="2">Cause</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            On a static page, the post's response hasn't started when your handler runs, so <Code>NavigateTo</Code> turns
            it into a redirect. Whether it stops your code depends on a project setting:
        </TextBlock>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>
                <strong>The .NET 10 Blazor Web App template</strong> sets <Code>BlazorDisableThrowNavigationException</Code> to
                <Code>true</Code>. <Code>NavigateTo</Code> records the redirect and returns, so the code after it runs.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Without that setting</strong> (an app from an older template, or one that added Blazor to an existing
                project), <Code>NavigateTo</Code> throws a
                <Code>NavigationException</Code> that Blazor turns into the redirect. A <Code>catch (Exception)</Code> around
                it catches that too, and the redirect is lost.
            </ListItem>
        </List>
        <TextBlock>
            On an interactive page, <Code>NavigateTo</Code> never throws: it starts the navigation and returns, so the code
            after it runs there too, whatever the setting.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Fix</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Call <Code>NavigateTo</Code> last, after the <Code>try</Code>/<Code>catch</Code> that guards your work.
            <Code>return</Code> right after any <Code>NavigateTo</Code> that isn't the last line, and on any path that
            shouldn't redirect. That works in every mode, with the setting on or off:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="csharp" Title="Rename.razor" HighlightLines="6,16,19">@Fixed</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            Rename the project, then reload: the page shows the saved name from <Code>?saved=</Code> and doesn't post
            again.
        </TextBlock>
        <ExampleViewer Example="form-redirect-after-save" ModeSwitch />
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>GetUriWithQueryParameter</Code> keeps the rest of the address. A message that shouldn't be in the address
            can go in a cookie or in your own store instead, read on the next request.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Related</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/components/form">Form</Anchor>: posting and submitting.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/troubleshooting/state-between-requests">A value resets on the next request</Anchor>: why the saved name goes in the address.</ListItem>
        </List>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>

@code {
    private const string Broken = """
        private async Task SaveAsync()
        {
            if (!Model!.Changed)
            {
                // With the setting, this returns, and the save below runs.
                Navigation.NavigateTo("projects");
            }

            try
            {
                await Projects.RenameAsync(Model.Id, Model.Name);
                // Without it, this throws, and the catch takes the exception.
                Navigation.NavigateTo($"projects/{Model.Id}?saved=1");
            }
            catch (Exception)
            {
                _error = "Couldn't save the project.";
            }
        }
        """;

    private const string Fixed = """
        private async Task SaveAsync()
        {
            if (!Model!.Changed)
            {
                Navigation.NavigateTo("projects");
                return;
            }

            try
            {
                await Projects.RenameAsync(Model.Id, Model.Name);
            }
            catch (DbUpdateException)
            {
                _error = "Couldn't save the project.";
                return;
            }

            Navigation.NavigateTo($"projects/{Model.Id}?saved=1");
        }
        """;
}
